  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Interest Form Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is an interest form?\", \"a\": \"An interest form collects the names of people who want something before it is scheduled or built, such as an evening course or a new sports team. Next to contact details, it asks the few questions that show how much demand exists and where it sits.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make an interest form?\", \"a\": \"Decide what the replies should settle and set a threshold first. Then keep only the questions that move it. For the form with its own database and threshold alerts, describe it to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>.\"}, {\"q\": \"What to include in an expression of interest form?\", \"a\": \"Ask for a name and email, and put the opportunity in the form's title. Add the answers you need to judge fit, such as availability. An expression of interest comes before a formal application. Keep it short and say when people will hear back.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I find a free interest form template?\", \"a\": \"Form builders and template sites each offer a free interest form template, usually for student clubs or volunteer programs. Most give you fields only. Start from the one closest to your case, then add the question that decides whether you go ahead.\"}, {\"q\": \"What to put in an interest form?\", \"a\": \"Put the options you are weighing in the first question, such as three weeknights for a class. Every reply then counts toward one of them. Then ask what would stop someone joining, like price or start month. A league adds age group, and a new building adds unit size and move-in month.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are interest forms used for?\", \"a\": \"Schools use interest forms to see which clubs would fill, and leagues use them to size teams. Landlords build a list before a building opens. Brands gauge creator demand before a creator application goes public.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is filling out an interest form the same as signing up?\", \"a\": \"No. A sign up form books a place in something that already has a date and a capacity. An interest form comes earlier, while you are still deciding whether to run it and how big to make it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What happens after someone fills out an interest form?\", \"a\": \"The reply lands in a database and the counts update. If Emergent built the form, you own that database. Once you decide to go ahead, everyone whose answers match gets the news, often with a link to the <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/registration-form'>registration form</a> where they commit to a seat or a spot.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is an interest form a commitment?\", \"a\": \"No. A reply says a person wants to hear more, not that they will pay or show up. Expect fewer people to enroll than said they were interested, and set your threshold with that gap in mind. Emergent puts no cap on responses, which helps when only a fraction of replies turn into sign-ups.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many questions should an interest form have?\", \"a\": \"Fewer than you think. Contact details plus two or three questions that drive the decision cover most cases. Anything you need only from people who commit, such as a payment or a signature, belongs on the later form.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}